Durarara!! AU
Chapter 17 – Part 1
The next day was a day off from Raira, and Curtis treated it the only way he knew how: by putting boots on the pavement and letting the city move around him.
He was dressed down, or at least as down as he ever managed — black technical hoodie with red panel lettering, cargo pants strapped and buckled, heavy sneakers, long black braids falling neat against his chest. No school uniform. No sealed night helmet. Just a tall foreigner cutting through Ikebukuro like he belonged to the street more than any classroom.
He made it three blocks before the familiar van appeared in his peripheral vision.
It drifted closer than ordinary traffic required. The side door might as well have been vibrating with intent.
Inside, Erika lunged toward the window first.
“It’s him. Braids. Heterochromia. Protagonist posture. Saburo, pull up.”
“No,” Saburo said immediately. “We are not snatching pedestrians because you two have main-character fever.”
Walker was already halfway out of his seat.
“It’s not snatching, it’s opportunistic recruitment. There’s a difference. Also he looks like he walked out of a limited-edition figure line. Pull up.”
“I am not—”
Erika reached forward and physically pointed at the curb like a general ordering artillery.
“Saburo. Pull. Up.”
The van jerked to the sidewalk hard enough to make the suspension complain. The side door slammed open.
Curtis stopped walking.
Erika and Walker were already out of the vehicle before Saburo finished swearing under his breath. They hit him from both sides with the cheerful efficiency of people who had decided consent was a soft guideline.
“Get in.”
“You’re coming with us.”
“Do not resist the plot.”
Curtis looked from one to the other, then at the open van interior, long black braids shifting as he exhaled through his nose.
“…You people have no concept of a normal greeting.”
They still got him inside.
The door slammed shut. Saburo pulled back into traffic with the expression of a man reconsidering every life choice that had led to this exact friend group. Walker and Erika crowded the bench like they’d successfully captured a rare spawn. Kadota, seated up front, only turned enough to glance back once the van was moving again.
“Nightstar,” he said. Not surprised. Just resigned. “You’re a hard man to run into by accident.”
Curtis settled against the seat, mismatched eyes half-lidded, already accepting that his day off had been confiscated.
“That implies this was an accident.”
Walker grinned.
“It was destiny.”
Erika nodded hard.
“Destiny with good fashion sense. That hoodie is doing criminal amounts of work.”
Saburo muttered from the driver’s seat,
“I hate all of you.”
Kadota ignored them and looked at Curtis properly now, the humor thinning into something more practical.
“We need extra hands. One of ours is missing. Not ‘late answering texts’ missing. Taken.” A short pause. “If you’re the kind of man who only walks away from things that don’t concern him, say so now. If not… we’re making a stop.”
The van carried them deeper into the city.
Outside, Ikebukuro continued like nothing had changed.
Inside, Curtis’s day off had already become someone else’s emergency.
Curtis tilted his head slightly, long black braids shifting against the black technical hoodie as he looked around the van’s interior with mild disappointment.
“…You’re not taking me to a Ruri Hijiribe concert? Bummer.”
The effect was immediate.
Saburo’s entire posture changed in the driver’s seat. The long-suffering scowl vanished so fast it might as well have been launched out the window. His eyes lit up in the rearview mirror with pure, unfiltered joy.
“You know Ruri-chan?!”
The van actually drifted half a lane before he corrected it.
“She’s not just an idol — she’s a once-in-a-generation presence. Vocal control, stage composition, the emotional sincerity in Rain Tears, the way she holds a crowd without overselling a single note—” He cut himself off, then pointed one hand back toward Curtis while still driving. “You have taste. Actual taste. Do you understand how rare that is in this van?”
Walker blinked.
“Did he just emotionally recover in real time?”
Erika looked delighted.
“He did. One Ruri mention and Saburo’s whole personality got a software update.”
Kadota pinched the bridge of his nose.
“Please do not encourage him. We are in the middle of a missing-person situation.”
Saburo was already too far gone.
“Missing person or not, respect has to be paid where it’s due. Nightstar. If we live through today, I’m putting you on the fan club contact list. Vice president recommendation. Maybe.”
Curtis leaned back in the seat, mismatched eyes half-lidded, the faintest hint of amusement at the corner of his mouth.
“…Sure.”
The van rolled on, one driver suddenly far more motivated than before, and three other passengers realizing the foreigner had just accidentally found Saburo’s only reliable on-switch.
Curtis looked from Saburo’s newly energized reflection in the rearview mirror back to Kadota, the brief amusement already thinning into something more practical.
“So why come to me for this?” His voice stayed even. “I’m not an info broker of any sort. Why not contact Masaomi or someone?”
The van went a little quieter.
Kadota answered first, eyes still on the road ahead.
“Because Masaomi is already carrying too much where the younger ones are concerned. And this isn’t a conversation problem. It’s a taken-person problem.” A short pause. “We don’t need someone who can talk a street into behaving. We need someone who doesn’t freeze when it stops behaving.”
Walker twisted around in his seat, grinning despite the seriousness underneath.
“Also, we watched you rearrange a sidewalk full of people after taking a board to the head and two stab wounds like it was a mild inconvenience. That kind of thing tends to stick in the memory.”
Erika nodded hard.
“You’re already in the city myth layer whether you want to be or not. Strong. Calm. Hard to read. And you don’t smell like someone who joins things just to feel important. That’s useful.”
Saburo grunted from the front, still faintly glowing from the Ruri mention.
“Plus Masaomi would turn it into a lecture about keeping kids away from the dark. We need hands, not a speech.”
Kadota finally glanced back at Curtis properly.
“We’re not asking you to become one of us. We’re asking if you’ll help us get our guy back. If the answer’s no, we’ll drop you at the next light. If it’s yes… we keep driving.”
The van held its line through traffic, waiting on him.
Curtis leaned back against the seat, long black braids shifting over the black technical hoodie as he gave a short, humorless breath.
“Masaomi’s a hypocrite.” A beat. “So yeah. I’ll help. Run the plan back with me.”
The van’s atmosphere changed at once — less pitch, more motion.
Kadota nodded like that was the answer he had expected all along.
“One of ours, Kaztano, got taken. Not random street garbage either. Trafficking route. They’ve been moving people through a rundown hotel pocket on the edge of the ward. We got a partial lead on where they’re holding him, but the window’s short.”
Walker picked up immediately, counting on his fingers.
“Step one: confirm the location without spooking them.
Step two: hit hard enough that they don’t get time to move him.
Step three: extract Kaztano before the whole thing becomes a body recovery.”
Erika leaned forward, eyes sharp under the usual theatrical energy.
“We already reached out for extra support on the approach. Black Rider’s in play if the timing lines up. Your job is simple in theory and ugly in practice — stay on the breach team, keep anyone from putting a knife in our people, and don’t freeze if it turns into a hallway problem.”
Saburo glanced at them in the rearview mirror.
“And try not to throw anybody through the van after. I just got the interior clean.”
Kadota’s voice cut back in, steadier than the rest.
“We move soon. If you’re in, you’re in for the whole stop. No half-help once the door opens.”
He looked back at Curtis once more.
“Any questions before we commit the route?”
Curtis muttered under his breath.
“…Fuck.”
Four pairs of eyes turned toward him at once.
He dragged a hand down his face, then looked up again, mismatched eyes flat with reluctant acceptance.
“I’m trying to avoid three people today. The Black Rider is one of them.” A short pause. “And no, I’m not in trouble with them. If you need… I can text her.”
The van went quiet in a completely different way than before.
Walker’s mouth opened first.
“I’m sorry. You can text the Black Rider.”
Erika stared at him like he had just claimed he had the Moon on speed dial.
“No. No, hold on. People don’t text the Black Rider. People leave forum posts and urban legend threads and maybe a courier request if they’re connected enough. You do not just… open your phone and text the headless motorcycle demon of Ikebukuro.”
Saburo’s eyes flicked to the rearview mirror, Ruri-chan joy temporarily overridden by pure disbelief.
“What kind of day-off contact list do you have?”
Kadota studied Curtis more carefully now, the practical part of his mind already rearranging the board.
“…You’re serious,” he said. Not a question. A confirmation. “You have a direct line.”
The van hit a smoother stretch of road. Outside, the city kept moving like this was ordinary traffic. Inside, the foreigner in the black technical hoodie had just casually admitted he could reach one of Ikebukuro’s most unreachable figures by ordinary message.
Kadota exhaled once through his nose.
“If that’s true, Nightstar… then yeah. Text her. Tell her we’re moving on the window, and if she’s already in, we need the approach coordinated instead of accidental.”
He held Curtis’s gaze.
“And after this, you and I are going to talk about how a transfer student ends up with that kind of number.”
Curtis looked at all of them with complete, flat seriousness.
“You don’t have her number?”
The silence that followed was almost offended.
Walker made a strangled sound.
“No?! Normal people don’t have the Black Rider’s number! That’s like asking why we don’t have God on LINE!”
Erika pointed at him hard.
“Exactly. She is an urban legend with a motorcycle. You request her through channels. You do not just… have her saved under ‘Celty’ between your dentist and your group chat.”
Saburo shook his head, both hands on the wheel.
“I’ve lived in this city longer than you’ve been standing in it and I still treat that helmet like weather. You don’t text weather.”
Kadota pinched the bridge of his nose again, but there was a reluctant edge of dry humor under it now.
“Most of Ikebukuro works through distance and rumor where she’s concerned. Courier requests. Indirect contact. If you’re close enough to message her directly, you’re already closer than ninety-nine percent of the people who talk about her online.”
He glanced back at Curtis.
“Which means either you’re unusually lucky… or you’ve been having a much more interesting few days than you let on.”
Walker leaned in, eyes bright.
“Text her. Right now. I need to witness this historical event with my own eyes.”
Curtis pulled out his phone.
The screen lit up on the most recent private thread — Celty’s messages from the night before and the quiet ones that had followed into the early hours. Not courier language. Not formal. A little too familiar. A little too soft around the edges. One of them bordered on playful in a way that would have caused an immediate riot if Walker or Erika saw it.
He angled the phone away from every pair of eyes in the van at once, thumb already moving.
Walker leaned in anyway.
“Let me see—”
Curtis shifted his shoulder and blocked him without looking up.
“No.”
Erika made an offended noise.
“He locks the screen like a man with secrets.”
“Because I have secrets,” Curtis muttered, still typing.
Curtis:
Van gang is moving on a trafficking hold.
One of theirs got taken. Kaztano.
They said you might already be in play.
If you are, they’re asking for the approach to be coordinated instead of accidental.
I’m with them.
He stared at the message for half a second, then sent it.
Only after the delivery mark appeared did he lower the phone slightly — still not enough for anyone else to read the thread above it.
Kadota watched him over the seat.
“Well?”
Curtis kept his eyes on the screen.
“Sent.”
Walker whispered, reverent and unhinged,
“He really just texted the Black Rider like he was confirming lunch plans.”
Curtis didn’t answer.
He was waiting to see whether the woman on the other end of the night ride, the underpass quiet, and the unanswered motorcycle emoji would reply as Celty — or as the rider who still didn’t know she’d already raced him.
Curtis glanced up from the phone and looked straight at Walker.
“Anyone ever tell you you look like Gin from Bleach?”
The van erupted.
Walker’s whole face lit up like he’d just been handed a lifetime achievement award.
“YES. Finally. Someone with eyes. The hair. The energy. The elegant menace potential. I’ve been saying this for years and these people treat me like I’m making it up.”
Erika immediately pointed at him.
“He does. He absolutely does. I’ve been validating that headcanon since day one. Fox-smile energy. Untrustworthy in a beautiful way.”
Saburo groaned from the driver’s seat.
“Please do not start the anime comparison game while we are driving toward a kidnapping.”
Kadota didn’t even turn around.
“If this becomes a full casting discussion, I’m putting both of you out at the next light.”
Walker ignored all of it, leaning toward Curtis with pure delight.
“Do I get the voice too, or just the look? Be honest.”
Curtis gave a small, flat shrug, already looking back at his phone.
“…Mostly the look.”
Walker still took it as a win.
The van did not go straight into a heroic breach.
It went looking for a missing friend the hard way.
By the time Kadota finished laying out what they knew, the shape of the problem was clear enough. Kaztano had sent a strange truncated text. His place was torn up. A kid named Hashim had seen a dark van and caught enough of the plate to matter. Three traffickers had been working the immigrant pockets near a rotting hotel strip: Asanuma, Morita, and Kanazawa. People who hunted the ones the city would not look hard for.
Curtis sat in the back in the black technical hoodie, long black braids neat against his chest, listening without interrupting. The day-off version of him was gone. What remained was the quieter, practical animal that showed up when someone had already been taken.
Celty’s earlier reply still sat in his phone.
Already moving.
Extract first.
Good. That meant the Black Rider was in the same weather system. It also meant the one person he had been trying to avoid today was now on a converging line.

They found the first trafficker because the crew had made a mistake.
Kanazawa had spilled the chloroform on himself during the struggle with Kaztano. Asanuma and Morita, getting drowsy and stupid from the fumes, had dumped him rather than keep a liability in the vehicle. By the time the van gang reached the area Izaya’s tip pointed toward, Kanazawa was alone, half-ruined, and still useful.
Walker spotted him first.
“There. That’s one.”
Saburo cut the van hard to the curb. Kadota was out before the engine settled. Erika and Walker moved wide. Curtis followed on the flank, mismatched eyes already reading the man’s posture for flight, weapons, or bluff.
Kanazawa tried to scramble.
He made it two steps before Kadota hit him like a door closing. Curtis caught the second motion — a desperate hand diving for a pocket — and put him down the rest of the way with a short, controlled slam into the pavement. Not showy. Just final enough that the man stopped thinking about running and started thinking about breathing.
Walker blinked at the speed of it.
Erika’s eyebrows rose.
Kadota only nodded once, already dragging Kanazawa up by the collar.
“Phone. Contacts. Meeting point. Now.”
The trafficker folded faster than dignity allowed. A meetup location. A window. The other two still had Kaztano.

The exchange point was an ugly patch of industrial edge-space, the kind of place built for deliveries no one wanted witnessed. Asanuma and Morita were there with Kaztano half-handled between them, already wary from the broken contact chain.
The van gang hit first.
Curtis stayed ordinary for the opening — public face, no sealed helmet, no Tech Rider myth — just another tall body in black moving with Kadota’s line. He took Morita’s wide swing on the forearm, stepped inside, and put him down hard enough to empty the fight out of him. Kadota smashed through Asanuma’s guard. Walker and Erika closed the sides. Saburo kept the van ready like a loaded exit.
Then the Black Rider arrived.
Celty came in from the far mouth of the lot on Shooter, yellow helmet low, shadow already answering her. The remaining resistance broke wrong at the sight of her. One of the traffickers tried to use the confusion to bolt with Kaztano. Curtis moved on pure reflex, faster and cleaner than backup was supposed to move, cutting the man’s angle off and ripping Kaztano free before the trafficker could turn the hostage into leverage.
For a split second, across the lot, the yellow helmet turned toward him.
Familiar motion.
Familiar force.
Wrong outfit.
Celty did not get time to interrogate the recognition. The last of the fight collapsed under the combined pressure of the van gang and the Black Rider, and then Kaztano was on the ground breathing, shaken, alive.

They kept Asanuma.
In the aftermath, pressed against the van and out of options, he talked because men like him always eventually preferred talking to what came next. The name he gave them was not a street name.
Yagiri Pharmaceuticals.
Not rumor. Not forum noise. A buyer. A destination. A reason people disappeared into the city’s blind spots and did not come back out as themselves.
The van went quiet around the word.
Kadota’s face hardened.
Walker’s usual spark dimmed into something colder.
Erika stopped treating the night like a storyboard.
Saburo swore under his breath.
Curtis stood a little apart from them, long black braids shifting in the industrial wind, mismatched eyes fixed on the captured man while the name settled into place beside every other ugly system he had ever watched consume people.
Across the lot, Celty remained mounted on Shooter. The yellow helmet turned once more toward Curtis — not long enough to be certain, not short enough to be nothing.
She said nothing.
But the private thread in his pocket suddenly felt a lot closer to the public version of the night than he wanted it to.

On the ride back, Walker finally said what the others were already thinking.
“You move too well for ‘just helping.’”
Erika nodded, still watching Curtis like she was updating a file in real time.
“That wasn’t civilian backup. That was someone who has done this before.”
Kadota did not disagree. He only looked at Curtis in the rearview’s edge and filed him, cleanly and without romance, under a new category:
Dangerous.
Reliable.
Not finished becoming a problem yet.
Curtis stared out the window and did not argue.
He had helped the good face of the Dollars without joining it.
He had brushed Celty in the field while still wearing his daytime name.
And the city, for the first time, had started placing him whether he consented or not.
Curtis said it the same way he said most true things — flat, unornamented, and without looking for approval.
“I would have killed them too if you wanted me to. Cover it up easily. Cockroaches like them are not missed or searched for long.”
The van’s residual noise died around the sentence.
Walker turned first, the usual anime-bright energy knocked clean off his face.
“…Okay. That’s not a hypothetical voice. That’s a man who has thought about the logistics.”
Erika stared at Curtis like the file she had been building on him just gained a much darker appendix.
“You’re saying that in the same tone other people use to offer help carrying groceries.”
Saburo’s hands tightened on the wheel.
“Please do not discuss clean body disposal in my van.”
Kadota did not flinch the way the others did. He only studied Curtis more carefully, the practical part of him measuring the distance between useful in a fight and willing to erase people.
“I didn’t ask for that,” Kadota said. His voice was calm, but harder than before. “And I’m not going to. We got Kaztano back. We got a name. That’s the line for tonight.”
A short pause.
“But I heard you.”
Curtis leaned back against the seat, long black braids shifting over the black technical hoodie, mismatched eyes half-lidded and unapologetic.
“I wasn’t offering sport. I was stating capacity. Men who sell people don’t become less dangerous because you leave them breathing. That’s all.”
Outside, the city lights slid by. Inside, the van gang sat with the newest version of him — not just strong, not just reliable, but someone who could talk about disappearance as a practical tool and mean every word.
Curtis looked around the van once, mismatched eyes moving from face to face with the same flat practicality as before.
“You four be careful who you ask for help next time.” A short pause. “If I was in a bad mood when I arrived, I’m sure you lot would have left me on the side of the road after the job was done.”
The words landed harder than the earlier talk of killing.
Walker opened his mouth, then closed it again, because the ugly symmetry of it was too clean to joke away. Erika’s expression tightened. Saburo muttered something under his breath that sounded half insult, half unease. Kadota did not look away.
“…That’s a cold way to read people,” Kadota said.
Curtis shrugged, long black braids shifting against the black technical hoodie.
“It’s an honest one. You needed capacity. I had capacity. If I’d been unstable, inconvenient, or more trouble than the rescue was worth, the smart version of this night ends with me becoming another problem you solve after the main one.” He glanced toward the window. “I’m not offended. I’m telling you not to get casual about who you open the door for. Not everyone who can help you will be safe to keep afterward.”
Silence held for a few seconds in the moving van.
Kadota exhaled through his nose, slow and steady.
“You’re not wrong.” A beat. “Which is exactly why we’re still deciding what kind of problem you are.”
Walker let out a low whistle.
“Man really helped us, threatened existential roadside abandonment, and still somehow stayed the most useful person in the vehicle.”
Erika crossed her arms, eyes still on Curtis.
“…Yeah. We’re definitely not dropping him off without a category update.”
Curtis’s expression finally sharpened, not into anger so much as open irritation.
“Then stop treating me like a problem.” His voice stayed level, but the edge under it was unmistakable. “You’ve been saying that all day. I’m a problem this. I’m this kind of problem.”
He looked straight at Kadota first, then the others.
“You’re pretty rude for someone who asked me for help.”
The van went quiet in a way that had nothing to do with traffic.
Walker held up both hands immediately.
“Okay. Fair. That’s fair. We did kind of collect you off the sidewalk and then start assigning boss-category labels like you were a loot drop.”
Erika winced, then nodded.
“He’s right. We’ve been talking about him like a narrative anomaly instead of a person who just helped us get Kaztano back.”
Saburo, eyes on the road, muttered,
“…He is still a problem. Just maybe we can say it quieter.”
Kadota clicked his tongue once and sat back enough to face the point properly.
“You’re right,” he said. No hedging. “We asked for help, you gave it, and we’ve been measuring you like a hazard rating ever since. That’s rude.”
A short pause.
“Doesn’t mean the concern is fake. You move like someone who has ended worse nights than this one. That makes people careful. But careful isn’t an excuse to keep talking about you like you’re only a threat class.”
He met Curtis’s mismatched eyes directly.
“You helped us. Thank you. We’ll stop talking around you like you’re a containment issue for five minutes.”
Walker leaned toward Erika and whispered, not quietly enough,
“He just made Dotachin apologize. That’s main-character behavior.”
Curtis leaned back against the seat, long black braids shifting over the black technical hoodie, and gave a short, unimpressed breath.
“…Better.”
Curtis looked down at his hands for a second, then back up, the irritation from before thinning into something flatter and older.
“I have a background with LA gangs and the cartel. Which is why I have my methods of dealing with these kinds of situations.” His voice stayed even. “Human traffickers usually end up in a ditch if they cross me. I’m sorry if my methods are also foreign to you.”
A short pause. The van’s engine filled the space he left.
“I’ve dealt with people who don’t give a second thought for the people they kidnap. In Mexico it’s awful. Most of the time you never see the full body returned from traffickers or kidnappers. I’ve seen families broken from those kinds of people. So that’s why I can’t stand them.”
No one joked this time.
Walker’s usual commentary died before it reached his mouth. Erika’s expression lost the last of its theatrical distance. Saburo kept driving, but his shoulders had gone tighter. Kadota watched Curtis in silence for several seconds, the practical assessment in his eyes changing shape — less hazard class, more a man carrying specific ghosts.
“…That tracks,” Kadota said at last. Quiet. No challenge in it. “The way you moved. The way you talked about leaving them in a ditch like it was maintenance. That isn’t fantasy talk. That’s memory.”
Walker exhaled slowly.
“Suddenly the ‘I would have killed them’ line hits different.”
Erika nodded once, more sober than she had been all day.
“Yeah. Hard to call a man overdramatic when he’s talking about partial bodies and empty families.”
Saburo’s voice came lower from the front.
“…Nasty kind of evil, traffickers. Different from regular street garbage.”
Kadota leaned forward slightly, elbows on his knees.
“We’re not going to tell you how to feel about people like that. Not after what you just said. But in this city, if we start solving every one of them with a ditch, the wrong kinds of attention come down fast.” A short pause. “You helped us get Kaztano back. That matters more tonight than how clean you’d have preferred the ending.”
He held Curtis’s gaze.
“I’m sorry you had to learn it that way. And I’m glad you were here.”
Curtis gave a small nod, long black braids shifting against the black technical hoodie, and looked back toward the window.
The van kept moving.
The name Yagiri Pharmaceuticals still sat with them.
But the foreigner in the back was no longer only a question of capacity. He was a man with a reason.
